354
00:33:32,156 --> 00:33:32,336
In

355
00:33:33,256 --> 00:33:35,696
1914, Sir Ernest Shackleton

356
00:33:36,496 --> 00:33:37,056
set sail

357
00:33:38,576 --> 00:33:42,196
to Antarctica for an expedition to cross

358
00:33:42,256 --> 00:33:43,596
Antarctica and to explore it.

359
00:33:44,616 --> 00:33:46,296
But in 1915,

360
00:33:47,576 --> 00:33:48,996
there was a disaster. They were

361
00:33:52,136 --> 00:33:54,376
stuck in the pack ice around

362
00:33:55,456 --> 00:33:58,756
Antarctica. They tried to free the ship, but they failed.

363
00:33:59,576 --> 00:34:02,956
And the pack ice really crushed the ship.

364
00:34:03,936 --> 00:34:06,716
So Shackleton immediately took action, and

365
00:34:07,696 --> 00:34:10,676
he abandoned basically the expedition, and

366
00:34:11,656 --> 00:34:15,116
he focused on saving his crew. He was sailing with

367
00:34:15,437 --> 00:34:16,296
27

368
00:34:17,736 --> 00:34:17,996
people,

369
00:34:19,417 --> 00:34:21,136
and they abandoned the ship, and they

370
00:34:22,096 --> 00:34:23,796
created a camp on the ice.

371
00:34:24,596 --> 00:34:28,196
And they stayed there for about four, nearly five

372
00:34:28,256 --> 00:34:30,716
months. But then spring arrived,

373
00:34:31,616 --> 00:34:33,756
and yeah, the ice melted,

374
00:34:34,756 --> 00:34:36,957
and, well, they had to move there because

375
00:34:37,996 --> 00:34:39,417
there was not enough ice left.

376
00:34:40,276 --> 00:34:44,156
So they took the

377
00:34:44,216 --> 00:34:48,116
small boats they had, the rescue boats, so

378
00:34:48,176 --> 00:34:51,776
three of them, and they set sail to the nearest

379
00:34:52,656 --> 00:34:53,056
island.

380
00:34:54,296 --> 00:34:57,996
That took six days, and then they arrived at Elephant

381
00:34:58,116 --> 00:35:01,376
Island, which was a small island, inhabited.

382
00:35:01,976 --> 00:35:04,236
But at least for the time being, they were safe.

383
00:35:05,836 --> 00:35:09,496
But Shackleton realized that there was no chance that

384
00:35:09,936 --> 00:35:11,216
people would ever find them there,

385
00:35:12,196 --> 00:35:14,956
because it was very far from the rest of the

386
00:35:16,376 --> 00:35:17,436
inhabited world,

387
00:35:18,776 --> 00:35:20,796
and they had no radio at that time, of course.

388
00:35:21,116 --> 00:35:21,356
So

389
00:35:22,276 --> 00:35:25,836
he decided to take five of his crew, take the

390
00:35:25,896 --> 00:35:26,636
smallest boat,

391
00:35:27,496 --> 00:35:28,516
and set sail

392
00:35:29,756 --> 00:35:31,356
to South Georgia Island,

393
00:35:32,256 --> 00:35:35,696
which was a journey of 800 miles.

394
00:35:36,556 --> 00:35:38,816
And it took 17 days. They only had

395
00:35:39,896 --> 00:35:42,596
very limited navigation instruments,

396
00:35:44,616 --> 00:35:45,376
only a sextant,

397
00:35:46,536 --> 00:35:47,516
but they managed to do that.

398
00:35:49,476 --> 00:35:53,236
It was very difficult to land there, because there was a big

399
00:35:53,276 --> 00:35:55,616
hurricane going on, so it took them two days.

400
00:35:57,436 --> 00:35:59,116
They suffered severely from

401
00:36:00,076 --> 00:36:03,816
frostbite. The water was contaminated with seawater.

402
00:36:04,216 --> 00:36:05,756
So they were in pretty bad condition.

403
00:36:06,356 --> 00:36:10,256
And they arrived at the wrong side of the island, at the south part of the island,

404
00:36:10,436 --> 00:36:13,036
where no people were there, and

405
00:36:14,016 --> 00:36:16,856
they had no chance of sailing further to the north part.

406
00:36:16,936 --> 00:36:17,096
So

407
00:36:18,096 --> 00:36:21,976
Ernest Shackleton, together with two of his crew who were in the best

408
00:36:22,016 --> 00:36:22,476
condition,

409
00:36:23,316 --> 00:36:24,216
went by foot

410
00:36:25,176 --> 00:36:29,116
to the other side of the island, to a whale station there,

411
00:36:30,216 --> 00:36:31,316
36 hours on foot,

412
00:36:32,276 --> 00:36:35,116
and they managed to do that. So they

413
00:36:35,916 --> 00:36:37,676
managed to get there, get help.

414
00:36:39,676 --> 00:36:40,596
And at the end,

415
00:36:41,416 --> 00:36:41,896
Shackleton

416
00:36:44,136 --> 00:36:46,916
created a new expedition to rescue the rest of his crew,

417
00:36:48,796 --> 00:36:49,476
which was about

418
00:36:50,996 --> 00:36:52,356
August 1916.

419
00:36:53,416 --> 00:36:55,296
So the crew survived for 22 months-

420
00:36:56,284 --> 00:36:59,484
On the island, living on hunting seals and

421
00:37:01,444 --> 00:37:02,984
drinking melting ice, et cetera.

422
00:37:05,024 --> 00:37:07,244
So all the crew at the end survived.

423
00:37:08,624 --> 00:37:09,064
And why

424
00:37:10,064 --> 00:37:11,964
am I telling this story now?

425
00:37:12,844 --> 00:37:14,444
Because this is all about resilience.

426
00:37:14,564 --> 00:37:18,244
Resilience is not about avoiding that crash that happens,

427
00:37:18,824 --> 00:37:20,704
but it is about taking immediate action.

428
00:37:22,284 --> 00:37:26,024
So he took immediate action to make sure that he saved his

429
00:37:26,064 --> 00:37:26,344
crew.

430
00:37:27,964 --> 00:37:31,204
And not only that, but it's also about never losing hope

431
00:37:32,044 --> 00:37:35,964
and about focusing on preserving your most precious assets, in

432
00:37:36,004 --> 00:37:38,844
this case, the crew. So it's all about resilience.

433
00:37:43,084 --> 00:37:43,304
Now,

434
00:37:44,804 --> 00:37:47,284
talking about resilience for our global community.

435
00:37:48,324 --> 00:37:51,784
Our global community faces a lot of disasters at the moment,

436
00:37:52,704 --> 00:37:53,004
and

437
00:37:55,304 --> 00:37:59,144
what is important is knowing what we are losing and where it

438
00:37:59,204 --> 00:37:59,784
is moving,

439
00:38:00,644 --> 00:38:00,744
and

440
00:38:01,744 --> 00:38:05,724
how to protect what remains. And specimens play an important role in

441
00:38:05,764 --> 00:38:09,704
that because specimens provide the evidence that you can use

442
00:38:09,804 --> 00:38:10,044
to

443
00:38:11,384 --> 00:38:14,024
see what the decline of species is,

444
00:38:14,964 --> 00:38:16,624
how they move over time.

445
00:38:18,764 --> 00:38:20,064
So they are important for

446
00:38:23,684 --> 00:38:24,504
biodiversity loss,

447
00:38:25,384 --> 00:38:29,364
but they are also important for climate change studies, because they show how

448
00:38:29,904 --> 00:38:33,064
species evolve over time and adapt to changing

449
00:38:33,364 --> 00:38:36,624
conditions. And also for health risks,

450
00:38:37,664 --> 00:38:41,224
because the specimens contain information about pathogens and the

451
00:38:41,244 --> 00:38:44,364
relationships with humans and other

452
00:38:44,724 --> 00:38:45,264
organisms.

453
00:38:49,224 --> 00:38:49,984
But there is an issue

454
00:38:50,904 --> 00:38:51,984
with specimens,

455
00:38:53,364 --> 00:38:56,444
because specimens are hosted in

456
00:38:57,564 --> 00:38:59,404
institutions scattered all over the world,

457
00:39:00,744 --> 00:39:01,744
in cabinets,

458
00:39:03,044 --> 00:39:05,684
stored, and it's quite fragile.

459
00:39:09,024 --> 00:39:09,264
So,

460
00:39:10,784 --> 00:39:12,024
we need to do something to

461
00:39:13,664 --> 00:39:16,884
make them better findable and

462
00:39:17,424 --> 00:39:17,824
usable.

463
00:39:19,424 --> 00:39:19,924
Also,

464
00:39:21,864 --> 00:39:22,844
the specimens

465
00:39:24,764 --> 00:39:25,204
data

466
00:39:26,544 --> 00:39:28,804
is scattered all over the place,

467
00:39:29,904 --> 00:39:33,464
and we need to make sure that data becomes

468
00:39:34,384 --> 00:39:35,204
better available.

469
00:39:41,024 --> 00:39:44,864
So the data is no longer a luxury. It's really necessary

470
00:39:45,064 --> 00:39:46,184
for our survival.

471
00:39:48,144 --> 00:39:52,044
Now, a challenge that we have is that in Europe, only

472
00:39:52,084 --> 00:39:54,784
10% of the specimens is currently digitized,

