00:59:22,100 --> 00:59:25,800
I'm also the co-PI of CESAAR, the Center for Earth and Extraterrestrial Sample

811
00:59:25,920 --> 00:59:29,000
Analysis and Archive, and the co-chair of the Physical Sample Curation Cluster in

812
00:59:29,040 --> 00:59:33,000
the Earth Science Information Partners organization, also known as ESIP.

813
00:59:33,140 --> 00:59:36,700
If you haven't heard of ESIP, it's the earth science version of TADWIG.

814
00:59:37,980 --> 00:59:41,640
In my talk today, I'll be discussing some data infrastructure resilience efforts in

815
00:59:41,680 --> 00:59:45,200
the earth sciences in the hope that this will contribute to future collaboration

816
00:59:45,240 --> 00:59:47,620
between the earth and biodiversity informatics communities.

817
00:59:48,280 --> 00:59:51,880
This is an admittedly US-centric talk, but I hope it'll still be of interest to

818
00:59:52,220 --> 00:59:54,260
folks in the international TADWIG community.

819
00:59:56,140 --> 00:59:57,220
So I'm going to start off with a quote.

820
00:59:57,280 --> 01:00:00,000
"It's hard to care for that much data, actually.

821
01:00:00,400 --> 01:00:03,760
And there's no money to do it. There's no money for maintenance. It's not sexy.

822
01:00:03,940 --> 01:00:06,060
Nobody's name gets put on it like the wing of a building."

823
01:00:06,940 --> 01:00:09,680
So this quote is from an interview study I published a few years ago.

824
01:00:09,800 --> 01:00:13,740
My collaborator and I were speaking with collections database managers at

825
01:00:13,780 --> 01:00:16,820
natural history museums and other kinds of memory institutions.

826
01:00:17,380 --> 01:00:20,600
We were interested in understanding how they sustain their databases over long

827
01:00:20,640 --> 01:00:24,220
periods of time. And the answer is they sustain them with great effort and very

828
01:00:24,260 --> 01:00:24,800
little support.

829
01:00:25,800 --> 01:00:28,580
I'm starting with this quote because it sums up the state of affairs for

830
01:00:28,620 --> 01:00:30,640
infrastructure maintenance, even in the best of times.

831
01:00:31,280 --> 01:00:34,980
We rarely have the resources we need to keep our data systems running well, and

832
01:00:35,020 --> 01:00:38,620
it's hard to get administrators and funders to see why data systems are important,

833
01:00:38,740 --> 01:00:40,260
because they just seem so mundane.

834
01:00:42,160 --> 01:00:45,440
This is partly because data infrastructure and the work of data infrastructure

835
01:00:45,520 --> 01:00:49,420
maintenance is, by its nature, invisible, even to those that benefit

836
01:00:49,460 --> 01:00:50,160
from it the most.

837
01:00:51,320 --> 01:00:55,040
So, in their book, "Sorting Things Out," information scientists Jeff Bowker and

838
01:00:55,120 --> 01:00:59,080
Susan Star talk about this. They argue that invisibility is

839
01:00:59,140 --> 01:01:01,500
a feature rather than a bug of infrastructure.

840
01:01:02,560 --> 01:01:04,120
Infrastructure is meant to be invisible.

841
01:01:04,180 --> 01:01:07,580
It's meant to be transparent, meaning that it can support use without notice.

842
01:01:08,720 --> 01:01:11,960
The consequence of this, though, is that it only gets noticed when it breaks down.

843
01:01:12,916 --> 01:01:16,036
So think about some of the infrastructures you rely on, on a daily basis.

844
01:01:16,156 --> 01:01:17,796
The roads you drive on, the electric grid.

845
01:01:18,116 --> 01:01:21,516
Most of the time we don't notice these until they break, until the road washes out,

846
01:01:21,716 --> 01:01:23,696
or until there's a power outage or something like that.

847
01:01:24,676 --> 01:01:28,076
For data infrastructures, I've observed that this can make it harder for them to

848
01:01:28,116 --> 01:01:29,776
get funding for ongoing maintenance.

849
01:01:30,256 --> 01:01:32,936
If they are functioning well, no one sees the need to give them money.

850
01:01:33,376 --> 01:01:35,776
If they break down, no one thinks that they're worth paying for.

851
01:01:36,416 --> 01:01:40,196
In other words, the invisibility of infrastructure, data or otherwise, constitutes

852
01:01:40,236 --> 01:01:42,856
a passive threat to its ongoing existence.

853
01:01:45,356 --> 01:01:48,916
However, over the last few years, two years specifically, data

854
01:01:48,956 --> 01:01:52,236
infrastructures in the earth sciences have started facing more active threats.

855
01:01:52,756 --> 01:01:56,376
In the US at least, our databases have become too visible to those who are

856
01:01:56,436 --> 01:01:57,996
interested in defunding science.

857
01:01:58,856 --> 01:02:02,816
These screenshots are from early 2025, and they're taken from the NOAA Notice

858
01:02:02,876 --> 01:02:06,296
of Changes page, which documents edits and deletions of NOAA web

859
01:02:06,336 --> 01:02:07,016
resources.

860
01:02:08,316 --> 01:02:11,616
For the international folks, NOAA stands for the US National Oceanic and

861
01:02:11,696 --> 01:02:15,476
Atmospheric Administration agency, which studies things like

862
01:02:15,516 --> 01:02:16,116
climate change.

863
01:02:17,276 --> 01:02:20,896
So these screenshots are listing over 20 NOAA databases and data resources that

864
01:02:20,916 --> 01:02:24,496
were taken offline following huge budget cuts and layoffs across

865
01:02:24,556 --> 01:02:27,836
multiple US federal agencies in the earth and environmental sciences.

866
01:02:29,356 --> 01:02:33,016
These cuts and takedowns have had a huge impact in the earth science informatics

867
01:02:33,076 --> 01:02:36,456
community. I've lost so many colleagues because of these, half the people that I

868
01:02:36,516 --> 01:02:39,636
used to work with at ESIP just lost their jobs and have had to go

869
01:02:40,536 --> 01:02:41,476
work in the private sector.

870
01:02:43,216 --> 01:02:47,036
And the takedowns of these data resources have led to multiple data rescue and

871
01:02:47,076 --> 01:02:51,016
data resilience efforts. We've been scrambling not just to archive data that we've

872
01:02:51,076 --> 01:02:54,396
spent actual decades collecting and curating, but we're also trying to replace that

873
01:02:54,456 --> 01:02:57,896
human infrastructure and that institutional memory that we lost in these budget

874
01:02:57,936 --> 01:02:58,256
cuts.

875
01:03:00,776 --> 01:03:03,596
So I'm going to talk about three of these data resilience efforts.

876
01:03:04,116 --> 01:03:06,676
Just three, but know that there are many more efforts beyond this.

877
01:03:07,296 --> 01:03:10,336
I've only been directly involved in the first and third of these that I'll talk

878
01:03:10,416 --> 01:03:13,236
about, but not the second. So first I'll discuss some data rescue

879
01:03:14,176 --> 01:03:17,536
work that we're doing at SESR. Second, I'll talk about a project that aims to

880
01:03:17,596 --> 01:03:21,576
identify near future threats to repositories, so the ESIP Repository

881
01:03:21,636 --> 01:03:25,476
Crisis Scorecards project. And finally, I'll briefly talk about a larger effort

882
01:03:25,536 --> 01:03:29,376
aimed at building shared social capacity for infrastructure resilience

883
01:03:29,816 --> 01:03:32,276
led by AGU, the American Geophysical Union.

884
01:03:34,596 --> 01:03:37,276
So starting off with data rescue work that we're doing at SESR.

885
01:03:37,356 --> 01:03:40,536
So SESR, again, the System for Earth and Extraterrestrial Sample

886
01:03:40,616 --> 01:03:44,396
Registration. SESR's been around for almost 20 years,

887
01:03:44,856 --> 01:03:45,736
in its current form.

888
01:03:46,736 --> 01:03:50,676
And it's a giant repository of metadata records describing physical samples, so

889
01:03:50,716 --> 01:03:54,336
things like rock samples, fossils, cores, things like that.

890
01:03:55,436 --> 01:03:59,336
SESR's biggest claim to fame is that it mints persistent identifiers called

891
01:03:59,436 --> 01:04:03,296
IGSNs for samples, which makes them easier to cite or reference in

892
01:04:03,336 --> 01:04:04,996
related publications and datasets.

893
01:04:06,996 --> 01:04:10,816
And we also offer some simple collections and metadata management tools.

894
01:04:11,816 --> 01:04:15,436
So the IGSN is basically a DOI at this point, and there's a long history to that,

895
01:04:16,256 --> 01:04:18,976
and it stands for the International Generic Sample Number.

896
01:04:19,776 --> 01:04:23,376
While SESR's holdings are mostly in the earth sciences, we're currently

897
01:04:23,436 --> 01:04:27,096
undergoing a huge redesign that will make it easier for people in the biodiversity

898
01:04:27,136 --> 01:04:30,896
sciences as well to use us, and I talked about that in the talk on Monday.

