912
01:05:19,636 --> 01:05:23,476
across the world. But the IMLGS was the only place you could go

913
01:05:23,556 --> 01:05:27,096
to do a federated search across all of these repositories at one time.

914
01:05:27,696 --> 01:05:31,355
Some of these core records are in SESR

915
01:05:31,415 --> 01:05:32,996
already, but not all of them.

916
01:05:33,816 --> 01:05:36,516
So when the IMLGS portal was taken down,

917
01:05:37,756 --> 01:05:37,956
this

918
01:05:39,296 --> 01:05:42,576
marine and lacustrine community lost a really, really important resource that

919
01:05:42,636 --> 01:05:45,556
helped them do their science and also helped them manage their collections.

920
01:05:46,636 --> 01:05:50,376
So what we're doing at SESR is basically trying to slowly

921
01:05:50,416 --> 01:05:52,856
bring this into SESR's collection.

922
01:05:52,936 --> 01:05:56,916
So bringing all of the cores that are not in SESR into SESR so that people can

923
01:05:56,956 --> 01:06:00,176
search those as well. We're doing this in two phases.

924
01:06:00,216 --> 01:06:01,536
We've already finished the first one.

925
01:06:02,316 --> 01:06:05,936
And the first one was to effectively just redesign the existing

926
01:06:06,036 --> 01:06:09,836
IMLGS repository within SESR as its own

927
01:06:09,876 --> 01:06:12,636
discreet little app, and that's what this second screenshot is of.

928
01:06:13,416 --> 01:06:17,376
Dave Viglase did this work for us, and he did this really excellent job with it.

929
01:06:17,436 --> 01:06:20,636
It uses a file format called GeoParquet that I'm not going to try to explain right

930
01:06:20,676 --> 01:06:20,856
now.

931
01:06:21,816 --> 01:06:25,316
But yeah, so phase one was just literally recreating

932
01:06:25,416 --> 01:06:28,056
IMLGS as it was in January 2025,

933
01:06:28,936 --> 01:06:31,736
within its own little spot on the SESR website.

934
01:06:32,176 --> 01:06:35,536
Our second phase, though, will be integrating all of these records into the SESR

935
01:06:35,576 --> 01:06:39,556
database itself and also doing some slight alterations to SESR's data

936
01:06:39,616 --> 01:06:42,336
architecture and vocabularies so that we better

937
01:06:43,276 --> 01:06:47,116
represent the IMLGS records. There's not a one-to-one crosswalk

