01:06:47,156 --> 01:06:51,056
between IMLGS and SESR currently, but over the next year, once we're done with

939
01:06:51,096 --> 01:06:51,656
our migration,

940
01:06:52,556 --> 01:06:55,616
we'll start working on this to actually bring those records into SESR.

941
01:06:56,056 --> 01:06:59,836
So that's our data rescue effort, and this is representative of a lot of similar

942
01:06:59,916 --> 01:07:02,756
data rescue efforts across the earth sciences, where people have been trying to

943
01:07:02,796 --> 01:07:06,456
bring shut down databases into still extant databases.

944
01:07:11,080 --> 01:07:13,760
The second resilience project I'll talk about is coming out of ESIP.

945
01:07:13,840 --> 01:07:16,480
So again, ESIP is the earth science version of TADWIG.

946
01:07:17,340 --> 01:07:21,180
So the Repository Crisis Scorecards project came about out of

947
01:07:21,220 --> 01:07:25,020
early conversations in ESIP about how to respond both to the

948
01:07:25,160 --> 01:07:28,960
2025 shutdowns and also to prepare for potential further

949
01:07:29,000 --> 01:07:30,240
shutdowns in the near term.

950
01:07:31,080 --> 01:07:35,020
In short, folks wanted a way of assessing repositories to know what

951
01:07:35,060 --> 01:07:38,020
was at the greatest threat so that they could be a little bit more proactive in

952
01:07:38,040 --> 01:07:39,200
their data rescue efforts.

953
01:07:40,040 --> 01:07:41,860
So this project was started by Joseph Gum,

954
01:07:42,920 --> 01:07:46,840
and it developed a system for analyzing data repositories to better understand

955
01:07:47,040 --> 01:07:50,800
how at risk they are. So this scorecard is basically a Qualtrics

956
01:07:50,960 --> 01:07:54,500
survey at this point, and it's meant to be a tool for self-study for

957
01:07:54,560 --> 01:07:58,120
repositories to go through and systematically assess their potential

958
01:07:58,500 --> 01:08:02,020
weaknesses according to four different crisis scenarios,

959
01:08:02,480 --> 01:08:06,200
including natural disasters, loss of funding, cyber attack, and loss of

960
01:08:06,240 --> 01:08:09,340
personnel. In some ways, this is similar to some existing

961
01:08:09,840 --> 01:08:13,460
audit systems and accreditation systems out of the library

962
01:08:13,520 --> 01:08:15,600
sciences meant to assess the

963
01:08:16,420 --> 01:08:20,221
stability of repositories, but this one is particularly focused on crisis

964
01:08:20,280 --> 01:08:22,521
response and preparedness for crisis response.

965
01:08:23,420 --> 01:08:27,260
So this scorecard project is all available on ESIP's website and in

966
01:08:27,320 --> 01:08:30,120
Zenodo. There's links in the lower left-hand corner that you probably can't

967
01:08:31,240 --> 01:08:34,561
see, but if you Google it, it's relatively easy to find.

968
01:08:35,260 --> 01:08:39,240
And the screenshot that's at the top of this slide is showing some of

969
01:08:39,280 --> 01:08:40,980
the questions that you go through in

970
01:08:42,920 --> 01:08:44,400
answering this survey.

971
01:08:45,900 --> 01:08:49,480
Finally, I want to briefly talk about a project started by the American

972
01:08:49,540 --> 01:08:53,080
Geophysical Union. So this is an infrastructure resilience and

973
01:08:53,120 --> 01:08:56,660
community building initiative funded by the William and Flora Hewlett Foundation.

974
01:08:57,440 --> 01:08:59,601
So this one is an international project.

975
01:08:59,760 --> 01:09:03,320
AGU has brought together repository managers across multiple earth and

976
01:09:03,360 --> 01:09:06,760
environmental science domains and multiple countries and continents.

977
01:09:07,860 --> 01:09:11,681
And starting with this great meeting that we had in Berlin back in

978
01:09:11,760 --> 01:09:15,580
April, AGU basically organized us into task groups

979
01:09:16,260 --> 01:09:19,200
that are developing longer-term roadmaps for data resilience.

980
01:09:19,960 --> 01:09:23,420
So these task groups are tackling a wide range of projects, including things like

981
01:09:23,460 --> 01:09:26,480
business model development and research, so talking about how

982
01:09:26,721 --> 01:09:29,980
repositories can diversify their funding streams and

983
01:09:30,820 --> 01:09:34,021
trying to do some research to understand what people are willing to pay for,

984
01:09:35,300 --> 01:09:37,800
working together to identify shared technical solutions,

985
01:09:38,641 --> 01:09:42,280
and developing things like repository crisis preparedness kits and

986
01:09:42,340 --> 01:09:45,660
building mutual aid networks. So getting repositories to

987
01:09:45,700 --> 01:09:49,380
agree to take on one another's data if the worst

988
01:09:49,420 --> 01:09:53,400
happens and they get shut down without having any additional time to

989
01:09:53,440 --> 01:09:57,240
prepare. So I was a participant in this kickoff meeting, like I mentioned, and it's

990
01:09:57,260 --> 01:10:00,020
my understanding that they would welcome participation from other domains.

991
01:10:00,200 --> 01:10:03,060
So please email me if you're interested in getting in touch with these folks.

992
01:10:04,660 --> 01:10:07,800
So to summarize, these three projects are addressing data infrastructure resilience

993
01:10:07,860 --> 01:10:11,680
across multiple timescales. So CSARS is more immediate, the Crisis Scorecards are

994
01:10:11,720 --> 01:10:15,180
more midterm, and AGU is aiming at a longer-term

995
01:10:15,440 --> 01:10:16,840
resilience efforts.

996
01:10:17,840 --> 01:10:21,760
To close, I'm struck by how all of these projects are about doing

997
01:10:21,800 --> 01:10:23,280
the best we can with what we have,

998
01:10:24,080 --> 01:10:27,660
or preparing to do the best we can with what we have.

999
01:10:28,420 --> 01:10:31,980
Often in standards and infrastructure development, we spend a lot of time talking

1000
01:10:32,040 --> 01:10:35,240
about best practices and lamenting about people who don't adhere to them.

1001
01:10:35,840 --> 01:10:39,680
But in these crisis response and resilience projects, what we see is that it's more

1002
01:10:39,720 --> 01:10:42,900
about doing the best you can at the time and working together in the future.

1003
01:10:43,580 --> 01:10:47,520
To quote librarian Trevor Owens, "Digital preservation is about making the best

1004
01:10:47,580 --> 01:10:50,800
use of your resources to mitigate the most pressing preservation threats and

1005
01:10:50,860 --> 01:10:51,220
risks."

1006
01:10:52,060 --> 01:10:56,040
I think this is a really helpful framework, and rather than aiming for

1007
01:10:56,080 --> 01:10:59,940
perfection, we're aiming for prioritization and just

1008
01:10:59,980 --> 01:11:01,240
