2361
03:05:48,142 --> 03:05:49,782
about what was going on.

2362
03:05:51,662 --> 03:05:51,902
So,

2363
03:05:54,602 --> 03:05:58,082
I'm not sure if this kind of data will make it in the final

2364
03:06:00,942 --> 03:06:02,682
paper, but it made us keep thinking.

2365
03:06:04,562 --> 03:06:07,682
The next thing to do was to look at books about the

2366
03:06:07,742 --> 03:06:11,362
analysis of biodiversity data. And here,

2367
03:06:13,922 --> 03:06:14,762
an important

2368
03:06:16,322 --> 03:06:19,282
differentiating perspective emerged,

2369
03:06:21,122 --> 03:06:24,882
and that is the difference between data coming from the museum

2370
03:06:24,942 --> 03:06:28,502
community and people analyzing that data, or data coming from

2371
03:06:29,482 --> 03:06:32,042
a biological survey community.

2372
03:06:32,962 --> 03:06:36,722
The data from a museum community is presence-only data plus

2373
03:06:36,802 --> 03:06:37,822
environmental data,

2374
03:06:39,602 --> 03:06:43,482
whereas the biological survey community had

2375
03:06:44,862 --> 03:06:46,622
a standard survey with

2376
03:06:49,362 --> 03:06:52,682
the scope of that survey well-defined, and

2377
03:06:53,982 --> 03:06:57,642
they had repeated surveys. And from that, all kinds of things

2378
03:06:57,722 --> 03:07:01,562
emerge. You have effort, and you can start to talk

2379
03:07:02,222 --> 03:07:02,802
about

2380
03:07:03,862 --> 03:07:04,862
detectability,

2381
03:07:05,682 --> 03:07:07,182
which is the key concept that

2382
03:07:08,662 --> 03:07:10,902
has helped clarify what's going on.

2383
03:07:16,922 --> 03:07:20,902
These two communities use different modeling techniques.

2384
03:07:21,262 --> 03:07:25,222
The museum community used Bioclim originally, and then GARP, and then

2385
03:07:25,282 --> 03:07:28,222
MaxEnt, whereas the

2386
03:07:29,562 --> 03:07:31,362
biological survey community use

2387
03:07:32,242 --> 03:07:35,982
standard population approaches, occupancy, habitat, and

2388
03:07:36,042 --> 03:07:37,242
hierarchical models.

2389
03:07:39,102 --> 03:07:40,302
And the results were

2390
03:07:41,402 --> 03:07:44,702
somewhat different. Museum community had niche models.

2391
03:07:45,142 --> 03:07:48,802
They looked at range maps and range extensions and habitat usage,

2392
03:07:49,842 --> 03:07:53,061
whereas the biological survey community was doing

2393
03:07:54,162 --> 03:07:57,742
abundance estimates in addition, and looking

2394
03:07:57,842 --> 03:08:01,802
at trends and habitat use. So they were taking

2395
03:08:01,862 --> 03:08:05,582
slightly different

2396
03:08:05,642 --> 03:08:06,082
goals.

2397
03:08:07,182 --> 03:08:11,122
The biological survey community is very interested in

2398
03:08:11,182 --> 03:08:14,342
management. They grew out of that kind of tradition.

2399
03:08:16,162 --> 03:08:18,582
And there are different references.

2400
03:08:18,722 --> 03:08:19,702
Here's just three

