2695
03:26:16,406 --> 03:26:20,046
The Australian Reference Genome Atlas, or ARGA, was the fruit of a

2696
03:26:20,066 --> 03:26:23,866
project that was undertaken in partnership between the Atlas of Living Australia,

2697
03:26:24,366 --> 03:26:27,386
the Australian BioCommons, and Bioplatforms Australia.

2698
03:26:28,166 --> 03:26:30,926
We also partnered with the Australian Research Data Commons.

2699
03:26:31,686 --> 03:26:35,006
The platform itself was part of the NCRIS community here in Australia,

2700
03:26:35,386 --> 03:26:38,266
providing free collaborative infrastructure for researchers.

2701
03:26:40,866 --> 03:26:44,366
ARGA, as we built it, was an index, not a repository,

2702
03:26:44,726 --> 03:26:48,506
bringing together complex DNA and genomic data sets from multiple

2703
03:26:48,606 --> 03:26:52,306
sources. It united them with their specimen occurrence data,

2704
03:26:52,606 --> 03:26:54,186
taxonomy, and other traits.

2705
03:26:55,566 --> 03:26:58,766
Genomic data are deposited into repositories using

2706
03:26:58,846 --> 03:27:02,626
INSDC standards, but not all of our candidate repositories

2707
03:27:02,746 --> 03:27:06,446
use these standards. Sometimes we had to deal with repository-specific

2708
03:27:06,506 --> 03:27:07,166
terms as well.

2709
03:27:08,066 --> 03:27:11,806
On the biology side of the equation, biodiversity data are shared

2710
03:27:11,886 --> 03:27:15,706
using Darwin Core standards, standards that are now routinely used.

2711
03:27:16,706 --> 03:27:20,686
Blending these two together was a big task, and to be fair, it's a task that's

2712
03:27:20,726 --> 03:27:24,686
getting much easier. Tools like the Darwin Core Data Package do a lot

2713
03:27:24,726 --> 03:27:26,966
of the heavy lifting to make the task simpler.

2714
03:27:27,686 --> 03:27:31,546
But a complication remains in the formulation of metadata indices,

2715
03:27:32,006 --> 03:27:35,086
and that is the singularity of the material entity.

2716
03:27:37,906 --> 03:27:40,126
The Darwin Core standard itself is very rich.

2717
03:27:40,526 --> 03:27:44,146
It can capture locations, geological context, taxonomy,

2718
03:27:44,346 --> 03:27:48,006
specimen occurrences. It even has the new extensions that try to

2719
03:27:48,046 --> 03:27:50,826
capture specific single genetic sequences.

2720
03:27:51,674 --> 03:27:55,634
But the model is also very flat, with one entity giving rise to one set of

2721
03:27:55,674 --> 03:27:59,474
descriptors. As you know, TADWIC has developed an event model to

2722
03:27:59,554 --> 03:28:03,274
overcome part of this issue. But when we commenced the AGAR project,

2723
03:28:03,654 --> 03:28:07,354
we found that we weren't able to capture all of the nuances we wanted for our

2724
03:28:07,394 --> 03:28:11,194
event types, and we also wanted to account for versioning in our

2725
03:28:11,254 --> 03:28:12,734
own indexation process.

2726
03:28:15,834 --> 03:28:19,514
As we saw in the previous slide, even using events, Darwin

2727
03:28:19,614 --> 03:28:21,694
Core works on the individual basis.

2728
03:28:22,274 --> 03:28:24,714
One entity produces one block of metadata.

2729
03:28:25,294 --> 03:28:28,614
But DNA, or more specifically, genomic data,

2730
03:28:29,094 --> 03:28:32,994
doesn't come from an organism. It comes from the amplicon

2731
03:28:33,114 --> 03:28:36,514
produced in a lab from a DNA extract derived from a

2732
03:28:36,574 --> 03:28:40,434
subsample of a specimen. It doesn't come directly

2733
03:28:40,454 --> 03:28:44,094
from the whole organism. It comes from the interpolated material

2734
03:28:44,194 --> 03:28:47,174
entity derivatives, each of which can be many.

