3532
06:04:02,240 --> 06:04:06,200
Okay, hi there. So I'm going to talk about the biodiversity literature as

3533
06:04:06,220 --> 06:04:09,920
FAIR and AI-ready data by design. So over

3534
06:04:09,980 --> 06:04:13,720
time, the publishing process has generated a very vast

3535
06:04:13,780 --> 06:04:17,320
corpus of taxonomic, biogeographic, and molecular

3536
06:04:17,440 --> 06:04:21,140
information. And in recent years, as demonstrated by

3537
06:04:21,200 --> 06:04:24,660
Guido, the community has developed increasingly effective

3538
06:04:24,740 --> 06:04:27,340
ways to liberate legacy literature.

3539
06:04:27,900 --> 06:04:31,640
But the central idea of my talk will be pretty simple.

3540
06:04:31,780 --> 06:04:35,560
The next step is not simply to liberate more literature, but it is to

3541
06:04:35,600 --> 06:04:36,940
change how we publish it.

3542
06:04:40,800 --> 06:04:42,440
So publications are not

3543
06:04:43,580 --> 06:04:46,640
simply the final output of biodiversity research.

3544
06:04:47,180 --> 06:04:50,860
They are part of the infrastructure through which biodiversity

3545
06:04:50,920 --> 06:04:54,740
knowledge is validated, connected, and reused.

3546
06:04:55,420 --> 06:04:57,980
A single taxonomic publication can contain

3547
06:04:58,880 --> 06:05:00,560
very rich information.

3548
06:05:01,600 --> 06:05:05,420
We are talking about taxon, material, sequences, places, people,

3549
06:05:05,540 --> 06:05:09,360
et cetera. And these elements are already connected conceptually,

3550
06:05:10,020 --> 06:05:13,880
but most of those connections are expressed primarily for human

3551
06:05:13,960 --> 06:05:17,920
readers. And the challenge is therefore not to only digitize

3552
06:05:18,020 --> 06:05:21,640
publication, it is to make these entities and relationship

3553
06:05:22,120 --> 06:05:24,180
explicit and machine actionable.

3554
06:05:26,760 --> 06:05:30,500
So some projects such as B-cycle have demonstrated that

3555
06:05:30,720 --> 06:05:33,340
biodiversity data can be liberated from publication.

3556
06:05:33,960 --> 06:05:37,640
Taxonomic treatment, material citation, and references can be enriched and

3557
06:05:37,720 --> 06:05:40,740
connected, and the resulting data can be pushed to

3558
06:05:42,060 --> 06:05:45,540
data infrastructures such as Treatment Bank and outward GBIF.

3559
06:05:46,260 --> 06:05:49,300
Up to date, as mentioned by Guido just before me, Plazi

3560
06:05:49,380 --> 06:05:53,160
liberated more than one million treatment from Legacy.

3561
06:05:54,340 --> 06:05:57,840
So the question is no longer whether we can liberate the data.

3562
06:05:58,220 --> 06:06:01,840
We can, but should this remain the way we actually publish?

3563
06:06:04,040 --> 06:06:07,920
If we continue to publish primarily as PDFs and then

3564
06:06:07,980 --> 06:06:09,360
extract data afterwards,

3565
06:06:10,340 --> 06:06:13,180
we will continuously create new material that

3566
06:06:14,080 --> 06:06:17,780
needs to be mined, then annotated and quality

3567
06:06:17,880 --> 06:06:21,420
controlled, and it is very resource-consuming, and

3568
06:06:21,620 --> 06:06:24,940
errors can occur. So there is an important

3569
06:06:24,980 --> 06:06:28,460
distinction to be made between the legacy issue and the

3570
06:06:28,500 --> 06:06:31,720
prospective one. For the legacy extraction remains

3571
06:06:32,260 --> 06:06:35,740
pretty variable, but for new research, we can move

3572
06:06:36,220 --> 06:06:39,900
the creation of machine-readable structure into the publishing workflow itself.

3573
06:06:40,740 --> 06:06:42,900
The objective is therefore not to

3574
06:06:43,860 --> 06:06:47,620
replace legacy liberation, but it is to complement it with

3575
06:06:47,700 --> 06:06:48,540
a prospective

3576
06:06:49,900 --> 06:06:52,140
approach that does not create the same backlog.

3577
06:06:53,600 --> 06:06:57,260
So instead of producing a PDF, we can work from a structured source.

3578
06:06:57,860 --> 06:07:01,620
In XML-first workflows, the structure of the article is captured

3579
06:07:01,640 --> 06:07:05,540
during the production of this article, and the same source can then

3580
06:07:05,580 --> 06:07:07,220
generate many outputs

3581
06:07:08,200 --> 06:07:11,300
for human and machines, PDF, HTML,

3582
06:07:12,360 --> 06:07:14,380
data representations, et cetera.

3583
06:07:14,760 --> 06:07:18,440
And the important point is not the XML as a file

3584
06:07:18,480 --> 06:07:22,140
format in itself, but it is that the machine-readable structure

3585
06:07:22,780 --> 06:07:26,556
exists right from the start. And this is the logic

3586
06:07:26,776 --> 06:07:27,236
behind

3587
06:07:28,596 --> 06:07:32,316
tools like ARFA, MitoTaxa, and the single source publishing approach.

3588
06:07:33,136 --> 06:07:34,936
For example, in MitoTaxa,

3589
06:07:36,396 --> 06:07:40,076
we have the manuscript that is structured and converted in XML TI,

3590
06:07:40,376 --> 06:07:43,636
then annotated in JATS TaxPub through GoldenGate,

3591
06:07:44,316 --> 06:07:44,516
and

3592
06:07:45,936 --> 06:07:49,656
then the main file, the pivot file, can be exported

3593
06:07:49,736 --> 06:07:53,176
to the outputs I talked about already.

3594
06:07:55,496 --> 06:07:56,936
So the first

3595
06:07:57,876 --> 06:08:01,636
structural level is metadata, title, authors, journal, and

3596
06:08:01,816 --> 06:08:03,356
other bibliographic information.

3597
06:08:03,916 --> 06:08:06,316
And this is important because metadata

3598
06:08:07,116 --> 06:08:10,356
is what makes the publication discoverable and identifiable

3599
06:08:10,876 --> 06:08:11,696
across systems.

3600
06:08:13,636 --> 06:08:16,456
We consider it not the administrative

3601
06:08:17,296 --> 06:08:21,276
overhead at the end of the process, but it is part of the data

3602
06:08:21,316 --> 06:08:22,576
that are being produced.

3603
06:08:25,596 --> 06:08:26,076
Also,

3604
06:08:26,996 --> 06:08:27,796
the references

3605
06:08:29,056 --> 06:08:32,656
are often treated as the bibliography, again, at the end of an article.

3606
06:08:33,056 --> 06:08:36,496
But in a machine actionable workflow, they are considered to be a structural

3607
06:08:36,956 --> 06:08:39,796
object. A reference can be decomposed into pieces.

3608
06:08:40,236 --> 06:08:44,216
Once structured, it can be matched against other records, and when a DOI

3609
06:08:44,276 --> 06:08:47,056
is available, a resolve to the referenced object.

3610
06:08:47,556 --> 06:08:51,296
And this matters especially in biodiversity knowledge, because references connect

3611
06:08:51,356 --> 06:08:53,936
to previous works, previous treatments.

3612
06:08:54,616 --> 06:08:58,596
A structured reference can then preserve a relationship that is valuable

3613
06:08:58,636 --> 06:09:00,356
for building the knowledge graph.

3614
06:09:02,896 --> 06:09:06,096
So however, structure alone is not enough.

3615
06:09:06,356 --> 06:09:09,076
A machine can read XML without

3616
06:09:09,836 --> 06:09:13,076
necessarily understanding what information means.

3617
06:09:13,556 --> 06:09:16,756
And in MitoTaxa, for example, the XML

3618
06:09:16,816 --> 06:09:19,936
TI file is annotated in JATS TaxPub

3619
06:09:20,676 --> 06:09:22,956
using the PLAZI GoldenGate. For example,

3620
06:09:23,896 --> 06:09:27,136
material citation is no longer a paragraph of text.

3621
06:09:27,196 --> 06:09:30,936
We can identify a taxon, a specimen, a locality, a

3622
06:09:31,036 --> 06:09:32,556
date, a collector, et cetera.

3623
06:09:34,656 --> 06:09:35,676
The material citation,

3624
06:09:37,656 --> 06:09:41,236
in this way, we can express relationships such as treatment describing a

3625
06:09:41,616 --> 06:09:45,356
taxon, a treatment citing a specimen, or a specimen being deposited in a

3626
06:09:45,376 --> 06:09:49,156
collection, just in this little paragraph of material

3627
06:09:49,216 --> 06:09:53,156
citation. Semantic markup therefore make entities and

3628
06:09:53,696 --> 06:09:57,116
relationship pretty explicit. And once these are

3629
06:09:57,176 --> 06:10:00,536
explicit, we need a common way of

3630
06:10:00,736 --> 06:10:04,396
describing them. And now I'm going to talk about the

3631
06:10:04,716 --> 06:10:08,596
grammar of interoperability, because indeed, machine-readable

3632
06:10:08,816 --> 06:10:11,016
does not automatically mean interoperable.

3633
06:10:11,196 --> 06:10:15,036
And two system can both produce XML and still use different

3634
06:10:15,256 --> 06:10:16,756
structure or meanings.

3635
06:10:17,776 --> 06:10:18,616
And this is why

3636
06:10:19,496 --> 06:10:23,196
standards are essential. For example, JATS TaxPub provides a

3637
06:10:23,216 --> 06:10:27,156
structured framework for publishing and annotating taxonomic content within JATS,

3638
06:10:27,796 --> 06:10:29,756
and Darwin Core provides a shared vocabulary.

3639
06:10:31,136 --> 06:10:31,856
Ontologies

3640
06:10:33,736 --> 06:10:36,716
also actually add further shared semantics where needed.

3641
06:10:36,896 --> 06:10:40,776
And the important point is that the standards provide a common grammar.

3642
06:10:40,876 --> 06:10:44,636
They allow us publishers, but also repository and

3643
06:10:44,696 --> 06:10:47,936
biodiversity infrastructure to interpret information consistently.

3644
06:10:48,436 --> 06:10:51,616
Standards allow that meaning to travel across system.

3645
06:10:52,496 --> 06:10:56,416
But even when two system use the same vocabulary, we still need

3646
06:10:56,456 --> 06:10:58,736
to know exactly which entity we mean.

3647
06:10:59,396 --> 06:11:00,276
And for that,

3648
06:11:02,056 --> 06:11:05,896
we are using PIDs, so persistent identifier, but

3649
06:11:06,076 --> 06:11:06,576
you know of it,

3650
06:11:07,596 --> 06:11:10,076
help us identify exactly which thing we mean.

3651
06:11:11,056 --> 06:11:14,936
For example, a publication can have a DOI, an author,

3652
06:11:15,056 --> 06:11:15,656
an ORCID,

3653
06:11:16,476 --> 06:11:17,716
a collection, GeoCycle.

3654
06:11:19,276 --> 06:11:23,076
This distinction matters because a name written as text is not

3655
06:11:23,656 --> 06:11:25,436
necessarily a unique identity.

3656
06:11:27,316 --> 06:11:29,956
But PIDs provide stable, resolvable

3657
06:11:32,356 --> 06:11:35,596
identities that can be used across a system.

3658
06:11:36,116 --> 06:11:39,756
And importantly, in the case of a workflow and

3659
06:11:40,056 --> 06:11:42,355
in general, PIDs are not just links.

3660
06:11:42,615 --> 06:11:46,276
The value come from being used consistently from a system to another.

3661
06:11:49,116 --> 06:11:52,816
So the objective, of course, is not to simply produce a

3662
06:11:52,876 --> 06:11:55,636
structured article and a long list of IDs.

3663
06:11:56,356 --> 06:12:00,316
The real value appears when precise entities and their relationships are explicit.

3664
06:12:00,856 --> 06:12:04,456
A publication can describe a taxon, cite a previous treatment,

3665
06:12:04,676 --> 06:12:08,076
cite examined specimen, connect those specimen to collection, connect

3666
06:12:08,396 --> 06:12:10,316
authors to institution, et cetera.

3667
06:12:11,016 --> 06:12:14,896
But when these relationship are expressed properly, the publication becomes

3668
06:12:14,956 --> 06:12:18,896
part of an interoperable knowledge network.

3669
06:12:22,036 --> 06:12:25,716
And at this point, we can see that FAIR can be designed

3670
06:12:25,996 --> 06:12:27,656
into publishing workflow itself.

3671
06:12:28,836 --> 06:12:32,076
Findability comes from rich metadata and PIDs.

3672
06:12:32,876 --> 06:12:34,036
Accessibility depends on

3673
06:12:35,016 --> 06:12:38,296
open resources and resolvable identifiers.

3674
06:12:39,156 --> 06:12:41,976
Interoperability depends on shared standards and

3675
06:12:42,556 --> 06:12:46,296
vocabularies, and reusability depend on semantics, provenance,

3676
06:12:46,796 --> 06:12:49,616
and also appropriately sensing. It is really important.

3677
06:12:50,856 --> 06:12:54,096
The key point is that this property reinforce each other.

3678
06:12:54,596 --> 06:12:57,796
They are not separate technical

3679
06:12:57,956 --> 06:13:01,576
embellishment, but together they create the condition for fair

3680
06:13:01,636 --> 06:13:01,976
reuse.

3681
06:13:04,396 --> 06:13:04,616
And

3682
06:13:06,156 --> 06:13:09,656
when many publication are produced and connected in this way,

3683
06:13:10,376 --> 06:13:11,816
they become part of a wider

3684
06:13:12,796 --> 06:13:16,496
biodiversity information ecosystem, what we call the

3685
06:13:16,516 --> 06:13:20,356
Libroscope. And semantically enhanced publication

3686
06:13:20,476 --> 06:13:24,316
can contribute data to resources such as

3687
06:13:24,356 --> 06:13:27,836
treatment bank and connect outward to GBIF collection,

3688
06:13:28,156 --> 06:13:28,656
taxonomic

3689
06:13:30,040 --> 06:13:34,020
database and sequence database. And the value comes from the network of

3690
06:13:34,060 --> 06:13:35,900
connection between this infrastructure.

3691
06:13:37,100 --> 06:13:37,660
This is the

3692
06:13:38,540 --> 06:13:42,180
broader vision. Publication become active contributor to the

3693
06:13:42,520 --> 06:13:45,160
interconnected biodiversity knowledge infrastructure.

3694
06:13:47,260 --> 06:13:47,480
And

3695
06:13:48,420 --> 06:13:50,940
this leads us to the AI-ready topic.

3696
06:13:52,520 --> 06:13:56,160
So AI-ready does not simply mean that an AI can

3697
06:13:56,220 --> 06:14:00,180
ingest a PDF. A PDF gives a model text, but a relationship

3698
06:14:00,220 --> 06:14:01,400
may be implicit,

3699
06:14:02,320 --> 06:14:06,220
entity identity can be ambiguous, and provenance and

3700
06:14:06,260 --> 06:14:09,640
context may be difficult to reconstruct.

3701
06:14:10,640 --> 06:14:13,860
But a semantically published article can provide this.

3702
06:14:14,380 --> 06:14:17,980
It can instead expose explicit meanings, entities,

3703
06:14:18,340 --> 06:14:22,180
relationship, and provenance. And this does

3704
06:14:22,240 --> 06:14:26,100
not remove the need for AI models. It gives them much

3705
06:14:26,140 --> 06:14:28,060
better structured material to work with.

3706
06:14:29,260 --> 06:14:32,880
AI readiness is therefore a consequence

3707
06:14:32,960 --> 06:14:34,840
of good semantic publishing.

3708
06:14:36,520 --> 06:14:36,700
But

3709
06:14:38,500 --> 06:14:42,340
to conclude, the real challenge is no longer proving that this is possible

3710
06:14:42,380 --> 06:14:46,080
for one article. The challenge is making it work at

3711
06:14:46,180 --> 06:14:46,580
scale.

3712
06:14:47,400 --> 06:14:51,000
We need to move from individual articles or individual publisher

3713
06:14:51,820 --> 06:14:52,860
towards a broader

3714
06:14:53,680 --> 06:14:57,320
convergence in the community. That means making standards,

3715
06:14:57,680 --> 06:15:01,320
PIDs, production workflow, part of the

3716
06:15:01,900 --> 06:15:04,120
normal academic publishing practice.

3717
06:15:04,660 --> 06:15:08,480
The legacy literature will continue to require extraction, but

3718
06:15:08,540 --> 06:15:12,060
every new publication can avoid adding to the backlog we already

3719
06:15:12,100 --> 06:15:12,540
mentioned.

3720
06:15:14,320 --> 06:15:18,220
So the message I would like to leave you with is, don't

3721
06:15:18,280 --> 06:15:22,160
just liberate biodiversity literature, but publish

3722
06:15:22,240 --> 06:15:23,940
it liberated. Thank you.

